# The .dag Format — Formal Specification v1.3

The `.dag` file format is a compiled, machine-optimized representation of a spec genome. It is the output of `dotdog compile` — a Directed Acyclic Graph encoded as a JSON document. No prose. No markdown. Pure structure. Designed for AI agents to traverse with zero token waste. Each .dag proves its own integrity and efficiency.

## Grammar (JSON Schema)

```
dag-file ::= {
  "version": "1.3",
  "project": string,
  "compiled_at": ISO8601,
  "compiler": string,           // dotdog version that produced this
  "integrity": integrity,
  "tokens": token_report,
  "nodes": [node, ...],
  "edges": [edge, ...]
}

integrity ::= {
  "sha256": string,             // hash of all source .dog files concatenated
  "source_files": number,
  "source_bytes": number
}

token_report ::= {
  "method": "chars/4",          // estimation method (4 chars ≈ 1 token)
  "source_total": number,       // estimated tokens in source .dog files
  "dag_total": number,          // estimated tokens in this .dag
  "savings_pct": number,        // percentage saved (e.g. 87.7)
  "savings_tokens": number      // absolute tokens saved
}

node ::= {
  "id": string,
  "type": string,               // entity, event, prediction, relationship, vector
  "category": string,           // entity | event | prediction — canonical category
  "description": string,        // for embedding and semantic search
  "file": string,               // source .dog file
  "properties": {               // full property schema (not count)
    "<name>": {
      "type": string,           // string, number, boolean, enum, json
      "required": boolean,
      "values": [string, ...],  // only for enum type
      "format": string,         // uuid, email, etc.
      "default": any,           // default value if set
      "constraints": string     // validation constraint description
    }
  },
  "states": [string, ...],
  "lifecycle": [string, ...]    // state transitions (e.g. "created → updated")
}

edge ::= {
  "source": string,
  "target": string,
  "verb": string,               // produces, contains, depends_on, etc.
  "description": string,        // prose description of relationship
  "cardinality": "1:1" | "1:N" | "N:1" | "N:M",
  "required": boolean,
  "file": string
}
```

## Validation Rules

### MUST (critical — graph is invalid)

| Rule | Check |
|------|-------|
| R1 | Valid JSON, parseable without error |
| R2 | `version` field present and equals "1.3" |
| R3 | `project` field is non-empty string |
| R4 | `integrity.sha256` is a 64-char hex string |
| R5 | Every edge `source` references an existing node `id` |
| R6 | Every edge `target` references an existing node `id` |
| R7 | Graph is acyclic — no circular dependencies |

### SHOULD (warning — graph is incomplete)

| Rule | Check |
|------|-------|
| R8 | Every node has a non-empty `description` |
| R9 | `tokens.savings_pct` is > 0 (graph is smaller than source) |
| R10 | At least one edge connects nodes |
| R11 | `tokens.method` is present (transparency) |

## Integrity Model

The `integrity` block proves the .dag was compiled from specific source files:

1. SHA256 hash of all .dog file contents (newline-separated) in sorted filename order
2. Source file count and total bytes

An AI agent receiving a .dag can:
- Verify the hash against source files to detect tampering
- Trust the token savings claim because the source byte count is recorded
- Compare against a previously compiled .dag to detect drift

## Token Efficiency

The `tokens` block provides provable compute savings. Token estimation uses 4 chars/token (standard for English prose). The `method` field declares the estimation approach for transparency.

```
Example: spec-platform
  method:          chars/4
  source_total:    11,712 tokens (46,848 bytes / 4)
  dag_total:       1,444 tokens
  savings_pct:     87.7%
  savings_tokens:  10,268 tokens saved per agent query

At $0.15/1K tokens (GPT-4o): ~$1.54 saved per session
```

v1.3 stores full property schemas (not counts), increasing dag size slightly but providing agents with actionable type data. The tradeoff is intentional: slightly lower savings percentage for infinitely more useful data.

## MCP API

`dotdog serve` exposes the graph to AI agents over stdio:

| Tool | Input | Output |
|------|-------|--------|
| listProjects | — | Array of project names |
| getEntity | project, name | Single node with properties, states, lifecycle |
| traverse | project, from, depth | BFS subgraph (nodes + edges) |
| search | project, q, type | Entities matching name or type filter |
| schema | project, entity | Property schema only — zero prose |
| summary | project | Node/edge/file counts, token savings |

Agent workflow:
```
1. listProjects → ["spec-platform"]
2. getEntity("spec-platform", "Payment") → {id, type, properties, states, lifecycle}
3. traverse("spec-platform", "Payment", 2) → subgraph of connected entities
4. Agent has exact types, states, lifecycle, and connections. Zero hallucination.
```

## Pipeline

```
.dog files → dotdog parse → AST → dotdog compile → .dag (v1.3) → dotdog serve (MCP) → AI agent
                               ↓
                         integrity hash + token report
```

## Conformance

An implementation is conforming if it:
1. Produces valid JSON matching the grammar
2. Includes `integrity` with valid sha256 hash
3. Includes `tokens` with source and dag token counts and `method` field
4. Nodes include `category` and full `properties` schema
5. Edges include `description`
6. Validates all MUST rules
7. Exposes the MCP API

